## TypeScript

TypeScript files also follow the JavaScript section; where the two conflict, the rules in this section win.

### Formats

- Keep imports single-line-per-module and grouped in sections: runtime built-ins, then third-party, then local, then relative, separated by a blank line.
<!-- Project-specific / TypeScript-Formats -->

### Names

- Use `PascalCase` for types, classes, enums, and enum members, named for what they are (`Order`, `PaymentGateway`); never with an `I` prefix or `Type` suffix.
- Name type parameters for their role when there are several (`TItem`, `TKey`); a lone `T` is fine.
- Use one discriminant field name (`kind` or `type`) across the codebase.
<!-- Project-specific / TypeScript-Names -->

### Packages

- Use `import type` (or `import { type ... }`) for type-only imports, so erased imports never turn into runtime dependencies; keep `verbatimModuleSyntax` enabled, and propose it where it is off instead of turning it on unasked.
- Use `paths` aliases only where the project defines them, written relative to the tsconfig; never introduce `baseUrl` for them.
- Keep `.d.ts` files for ambient declarations; types for your own code live beside that code.
<!-- Project-specific / TypeScript-Packages -->

### Types

- Keep `strict`, `noUncheckedIndexedAccess`, and `noImplicitOverride` enabled, and propose any that are off instead of turning them on unasked; never relax a compiler flag to silence an error.
- Use classes when state and behavior belong together, plain types and functions otherwise; prefer composition over inheritance.
- Use `private` visibility modifiers, never `#private` fields.
- Mark fields `readonly`, and type inputs as `readonly T[]`, wherever nothing mutates them; freeze literal collections with `as const`.
- Use `type` for unions and intersections; use `interface` for `extends`/`implements` contracts.
- Model alternatives as discriminated unions, and end every `switch` over one with a `never` exhaustiveness check.
- Prefer literal unions or `as const` objects to `enum`; under `erasableSyntaxOnly` or a runtime's type stripping, write no `enum`, `namespace`, or parameter properties.
- Use `satisfies` to check a literal against a type without widening it.
- Brand identifiers that must not mix: `type OrderId = string & { readonly __brand: "OrderId" }`.
- Avoid `as` assertions; when one is unavoidable, assert once, at the boundary, after validation.
- Never use `any`; take `unknown` and narrow it before use, and confine an unavoidable `any` to one adapter, with the reason beside it.
- Never suppress the type checker with `@ts-ignore` or `@ts-nocheck`; fix the types, or use a narrowly scoped `@ts-expect-error` with a *why* comment.
<!-- Project-specific / TypeScript-Types -->

